A||||7||XDrewX||? Move car without cradle ?||||5||
Z||000000||XDrewX||12-01-2001||01:31 AM||||There was a topic a while back discussing the ability to move the car (by hand) with the cradle removed.<BR>What ever became of this? is it possible?<P>Here is my challenge:<P>I need to remove the engine/tranny/cradle etc., from my car, then I need to move the car 6 feet back and turn it 90 degrees.<BR>This is due to my one car wide driveway, and the lack of a garage. I have a work shed just big enough to get the cradle assembly in and work on the eng/trans.<P>I will be replacing the nose at some point, so I was going to remove it now, so I dont have to put the front end on stands, I can just block the front tires, and be able to use those wheels to wheel the car around like a wheel barrow.  Am I thinking correct here ?<BR>Anyone done this?<BR>Can three pretty good sized young men move this car?   The seats will also be removed at this time, and the gas tank will only have about 2 gal of fuel.<P>Anyone ever done this?<P>Can the engine come out of the drivers side, or does it HAVE to come out the passengers side?<P>If it could come out the drivers side, I dont need to move the car by hand after I drop the cradle.<P>This may sound crazy to some, but I am very safety minded, and this is the only way to remove the cradle while the whole car is on a concrete driveway. <P>I just cant leave the car in the middle of my driveway all "naked", and leaving no room for other cars. The neighbors would B*tch.<BR> <P>My yard is very soft and I would never support a car on dirt or even asphalt for that matter.<P>Thanks<P>Andrew<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||watts||12-01-2001||02:19 AM||||I recently stripped down an '88 formula.  Even with the cradle out, the interior gutted, and pretty much everything stripped off - it was STILL a heavy pig!  I don't think you'd be able to pick the back end up and walk around with it.<P>I'd make up a 4*4 post rectangular frame, and slap some big casters under it.  Yank your cradle and do whatever with it, then lower the car onto the frame you just made.  Roll it away...<P>???<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||12-01-2001||08:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by XDrewX:<BR><B><BR>Can the engine come out of the drivers side, or does it HAVE to come out the passengers side?<P>If it could come out the drivers side, I dont need to move the car by hand after I drop the cradle.<P>Thanks<BR>Andrew</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>It can be removed from either side if you leave adequate clearance to do so. What are you going to use to lift the car to do your removal? The strut and spring assembly(s) can be removed also to achieve a slightly smaller 'lift' to remove it from under the car. The next ? would be, how do you intend to place it in the shed?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||12-01-2001||12:33 PM||||Got to Home Depot or Lowe's and buy a couple of furniture dollies. The rectangular wooden ones with 4 casters and the carpet attached for padding. You can also uses these, side by side with 2x4s laid across them, to get the cradle out from under the car. Once you get the cradle out, remove it from the dollies. You can then put the dollies and 2x4s under the rear of the floor pan (NOT the trunk. I don't think it will support the weight.), and should be able to roll the car around. You might have to use a 4x4, or drill and bolt the 2x4s together to get enough ground clearance, but I really doubt it. <BR>I just got finished stripping a wrecked Formula. I rolled the <I>entire</I> space frame (no cradle or front suspension) out of my garage into the driveway on two furniture dollies. <BR>Before you do any of this, please be on a flat, level driveway. Even a moderate grade can be dangerous. I don't want to hear about how a cradle, or Fiero on dollies chased someone down their driveway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">    <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||XDrewX||12-01-2001||01:21 PM||||Indiana_resto_guy:<BR>If It can be moved out of the drivers side, Than I am set.  I wont need to move car. All of the info I read on dropping the cradle, showed it coming out of the passenger side, so I assumed there was something like the gas tank filler tube that was in the way.<P>I am removing the cradle just like everybody else does, with the engine hoist (foldable type with wheels) and dollie method. I have some really tall (24") jack stands, that I will support car with as I use hoist to lower eng/cradle.<P>I can wheel the eng/cradle into the work shed, actually I can get the rear of the car in there, but it has sides, so I cant slide the cradle out thru the wheel well.<P>This is my first cradle drop, as you can tell, so I am taking my time and making sure everything will go off with out a hitch.<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||12-01-2001||01:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by XDrewX:<BR><B>Indiana_resto_guy:<P>I am removing the cradle just like everybody else does, with the engine hoist (foldable type with wheels) and dollie method. I have some really tall (24") jack stands, that I will support car with as I use hoist to lower eng/cradle.<P>Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hopefully you mean that you will lift the car off the cradle and not lower the cradle out of the car in the raised position.<BR>Raydar has a great suggestion if you need to move it around without the hoist. I never thought of using furniture dollies.<BR>Good luck!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DJRice||12-01-2001||07:23 PM||||We used one of those small Garden Trailers to do the same thing once.  It was busted up, but the frame and axle were in good shape and having those tires in the back sure helped.  <P>Otherwise, the dollies are the way to go.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Immortal||12-01-2001||11:42 PM||||I just removed my engine assy. <BR>If your using a engine hoist lower the cradel onto the legs of hoist.<BR>Hook chain in strut holes and pick car up off.<BR>Under the car you will see an indentation on each side of the car about where the jacking points are.<BR> <BR>Put your stands there, and you should be able to move everthing strate out the back.<P>I tryed the other way, looked to dangerous.<BR>Take the strut out with everthing else.||||reg||1
